INSERT INTO sessions VALUES('f785b9b7-636','/tmp','openai:ornith-1.5-35b','custom','ou are continuing the `agpack` project (portable, verifiable','builder',319,NULL,'[]',0,0,NULL,NULL,'sandbox-capability-engine',0,'2026-08-27 16:19:52','{}','{"boundary_index": 302, "summary_text": "[dcode-proxy: the model spent its entire token budget reasoning and never emitted an answer. Its reasoning is shown below. Raise max_tokens to get a real answer.]\n\nLet me carefully analyze this compaction task. I need to produce a structured summary of the conversation, folding in the previous compaction summary''s still-relevant content, and the conversation-since window.\n\nLet me trace through what''s happened:\n\nThe overarching project is \"agpack\" - a WASM tool sandbox (Step 2 of the project). The driver interface design was settled: host hands driver (module_bytes, imports, budget, audit), driver returns RunResult-shaped object.\n\nThe assistant has been building probe scripts in /tmp to validate the wasmtime 48.0.0 API and WASM module encoding.\n\nLet me extract the key technical findings from this turn:\n\n1. **Section ordering issue**: The type section count `vuint(3)` was leaking into section-header slot because the section wasn''t wrapped with section-id + size.\n\n2. **Memory section issue**: The memory section body must be `[count=01][flag=00][min=01]` = size 3.\n\n3. **Body size computation**: The key realization - hand-encoding section sizes fails because they don''t match actual bodies. Need to `section(id, body)` helper that computes size from body length.\n\n4. **Import count byte**: Import section needs leading `vuint(number_of_imports)` count, not the body length.\n\n5. **The walker trap**: The hand-walker blindly follows declared sizes, so it can''t catch a wrongly-computed size. wasmtime''s actual error messages are authoritative.\n\n6. **Memory import vs Memory section**: Every memory-section encoding fails while type+import (func imports only) parses fine. The realization: import memory from the host (no Memory section). The sandbox design imports `env/memory` anyway, and having both an import and a Memory section is a duplicate.\n\nWait, but then they hit \"exported function index out of bounds\" - the code section encoding issue.\n\n7. **Code body encoding**: The function body is just `locals` + `code bytes`, with no extra vector wrapper. So `00 0b` (zero locals + end) is correct, but they had a spurious `01`.\n\n8. **Function section (id 12)**: The code section requires a preceding Function section mapping each code index to its type index \u2014 \"function section is absent\".\n\n9. **Section order**: WASM canonical section order requires Code (10) before Function (12).\n\n10. **Function type index embedded in code body**: The current spec embedded the type index at the start of each function body in the Code section (or a separate Function section). wasmtime said \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\".\n\n11. **\"unknown function 2\"**: wasmtime counts fewer than 3 functions. Uncertain whether wasmtime wants typeidx embedded in code body or separate Function section.\n\nLet me look more carefully at the last few steps. The conversation got progressively more tangled. At the very end:\n\n- \"unknown function 2\" issue\n- They tried a clean matrix\n- Then switched to writing probe via `cat > /tmp/probe.py` heredoc\n- The realization: In the Code section, each function body is encoded as `[body_size][body]`, and each body starts with `typeidx + locals + code`. They''ve been writing `vuint(1)+func_body` but omitting the per-function body size prefix. Build exactly: body = `typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b)` = 3 bytes, wrapped as `count(01) body_size(03) body`.\n\nWait, `typeidx(02)` - that means type index 2? Hmm, let me re-read. \"body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body\".\n\nHmm, so they''re saying each function body starts with typeidx. Actually in the WASM spec, a function body is: `locals + code bytes` where the type index is NOT in the body. The type index comes from the Function section. But wasmtime 48.0.0... let me think.\n\nActually the previous compaction noted: \"the current WebAssembly spec removed that; the type index is now embedded at the start of each function body\". But that''s actually not correct per the WASM spec. The Function section (id 12) maps code indices to type indices. The code body does NOT contain the type index.\n\nHmm, but wasmtime said \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\" - which means wasmtime expected the Function section. So the correct approach is to have the Function section (id 12) with the type indices.\n\nWait, but there''s confusion here. Let me focus on what wasmtime actually wants based on the error messages:\n- \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\" \u2192 wasmtime WANTS a Function section (id 12).\n\nSo the correct approach: Code section (id 10) with function bodies = `count(01) body_size(03) body`, where body = `locals(00) code` = `00 0b` (2 bytes)... but they said \"body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes\". \n\nHmm, this is where the assistant is confused. Let me just note the current state: the assistant was in the middle of building probe.py with a specific encoding (body = typeidx + locals + code, 3 bytes, wrapped as count + body_size + body) when the context ended.\n\nActually, I need to be careful about what''s confirmed vs. what''s still being worked on. The last message from the assistant is:\n\n\"Now I see the real bug. In the Code section, each function body is encoded as [body_size][body], and each body starts with typeidx + locals + code. I''ve been writing vuint(1)+func_body but omitting the per-function body size prefix, and I mixed up where the typeidx goes. Let me build it exactly: body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nSo the assistant''s current plan is to build probe.py with this encoding. The `cat > /tmp/probe.py` command was shown but I need to check if it was actually run. Looking at the last entries:\n\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...type_sec += b\\\"\\\\x60\\\"+vuint(0)+vuint(1)+b\\\"\\\\x7f\\\" # t0 ()->i3\u2026\"\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...func_body=...}\"\n\"[assistant \u2192 run_shell] {\"command\": \"cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF''...In the Code section, each function body is encoded as [body_size][body]...wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nSo there are multiple `cat > /tmp/probe.py` attempts. The last one is the assistant narrating the fix but the command content is cut off. So the actual execution state is unclear - the last tool call may or may not have been run. It seems the assistant was describing the final encoding approach.\n\nGiven the truncation, I''ll note that the final probe.py via heredoc was being written with the \"body = typeidx + locals + code\" encoding, and its execution result is not shown yet.\n\nNow let me organize the wasmtime API findings:\n\nFrom previous compaction (still relevant) + this turn:\n- `ValType.i32()`, `ValType.i64()` \u2014 not `wasmtime.Kind.i32`\n- `FuncType([...vals], [...vals])` \u2014 note: results must be a LIST `[ValType.i32()]`, not a single `ValType.i32()`\n- `Config()`: `cfg.wasm_multi_value = True`, `cfg.consume_fuel = True` (property setter, NOT `cfg.consume_fuel(True)`)\n- `Engine(cfg)`, `Store(engine)` or `Store(cfg)`\n- `Linker(engine)`\n- `Memory(store, MemoryType(wasmtime.Limits(min, max)))`\n- `MemoryType(limits, is_64=False, shared=False, page_size_log2=16)`\n- `Limits(min, max)` \u2014 min, max are u32s\n- `linker.define(store, module, name, item)` \u2014 NOT `define_w_func`\n- `Module(engine, module_bytes)` \u2014 note `Module(engine, bytes)`, not `Module(store, bytes)`\n- `linker.instantiate(module)` \u2014 `lk.instantiate(Module(eng, full))`\n- `instance.exports` exists\n- `store.set_fuel(n)`, `store.get_fuel()`\n- `wasmtime.__version__` throws AttributeError\n\nWASM binary encoding:\n- Magic: `\\x00\\x61\\x73\\x6d`, version `\\x01\\x00\\x00\\x00`\n- Section IDs: type=0x01, import=0x02, export=0x07, code=0x0a (10), function=0x0c (12), memory=0x03\n- Each section: `section_id` (1 byte), then `vuint(len)`, then content\n- `vuint(n)`: LEB128 varuint\n- `name(s)`: `vuint(len(s)) + s.encode()`\n- `section(id, body)` helper: `bytes([id]) + vuint(len(body)) + body` \u2014 CRUCIAL to compute size from body\n\nFunction types:\n- `(i32,i32,i32)->i32` = `60 03 7f 7f 7f 01 7f`\n- `()->i32` = `60 00 01 7f`\n- `()->()` = `60 00 00 00`\n\nImport entry: `name(module)` + `name(name)` + kind_byte + kind_data\n- func import (kind 0x00): `vuint(type_index)`\n- memory import (kind 0x02): flag + min (min-only, unbounded: flag 0x00)\n- import section starts with `vuint(number_of_imports)` (count), NOT body length\n\nCode section:\n- count of functions + for each: body_size + body\n- body = typeidx + locals(00) + code... (this is what the assistant concluded, but see note below about confusion)\n\nWait, I need to be careful. Let me reconsider the WASM encoding. The assistant''s confusion:\n\nIn one step, they said the code body is `locals + code bytes` with no vector wrapper, and `00 0b` is correct (zero locals + end).\n\nIn another step, they said wasmtime said \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\" \u2014 which means wasmtime WANTS the Function section (id 12).\n\nIn the final step, they concluded: \"body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nThere''s genuine confusion in the transcript about whether:\n(a) Use a separate Function section (id 12) to map type indices, OR\n(b) Embed typeidx in the code body\n\nThe assistant flip-flopped. The message \"function section is absent but code section has non-zero count\" suggests (a) was needed. But then they went to (b) with \"typeidx(02)\" embedded in the body.\n\nHmm, actually \u2014 wait. Type index 2? That''s odd because there are 3 types (t0, t1, t2 = indices 0,1,2). Why typeidx 2 for all? That might be another bug. Actually, each function needs its own correct type index:\n- random: t0 (index 0)\n- emit: t1 (index 1)\n- run: t2 (index 2)\n\nSo \"typeidx(02)\" for all three would be wrong. But the assistant wrote it that way in the last message. This suggests the assistant''s final encoding is still uncertain/potentially buggy.\n\nActually, I should note the confusion as unresolved. The assistant was in the middle of trying to pin down the exact code-body encoding.\n\nLet me re-read the very last assistant message once more:\n\n\"Now I see the real bug. In the Code section, each function body is encoded as [body_size][body], and each body starts with typeidx + locals + code. I''ve been writing vuint(1)+func_body but omitting the per-function body size prefix, and I mixed up where the typeidx goes. Let me build it exactly: body = typeidx(02) locals(00) end(0b) = 3 bytes, wrapped as count(01) body_size(03) body.\"\n\nOK so this is the assistant''s current (possibly incorrect) understanding. I''ll capture it but flag that the typeidx(02) for all functions is likely a bug and the typeidx-vs-function-section question was still being resolved. Actually, since the memory is being handed off, I should capture the current working hypothesis precisely so the next session can test it, while noting the known uncertainties.\n\nHmm, but actually I need to think about what''s TRUE vs what the assistant thought. The key guidance is: \"", "working_state": "## Working state (extracted mechanically from tool records)\nFiles written/edited (most recent first):\n- /tmp/probe_final.py\nRecent shell commands:\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [ok]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [ok]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cd /tmp && python << ''PY'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break return   [exit 1]\n- cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break retu  [ok]\n- cat > /tmp/probe.py << ''EOF'' from wasmtime import * def vuint(n): o=bytearray() while True: b=n&0x7f; n>>=7 if n: o.append(b|0x80) else: o.append(b); break retu  [ok]\nTools used in the summarized span: run_shell, write_file", "user_messages": ["ou are continuing the `agpack` project (portable, verifiable agent runtime). Step 1 (artifact format) is DONE: schema.py, packager.py, validator.py are implemented and covered by tests in tests/test_artifact_bundle.py, which pass. Step 2 is the WASM tool sandbox (a capability-scoped execution engine for untru