reful. The example in the prompt \"never send without my approval\" is just an example format. But it seems relevant here \u2014 the standing rule is \"don''t commit without approval.\" Let me note it.\n\nAlso: agpack source is READ-ONLY. The assistant \"cannot run pytest (read-only, no shell)\" \u2014 but wait, actually the user said they ran pytest. Let me clarify. The assistant cannot execute pytest itself, but the user (or environment) can run it. Actually the user said \"Done. Both parts verified.\" and mentioned \"python -m pytest -q from the repo root: 203 passed\". So the user ran it. Or was it done by the assistant? Let me re-read.\n\nThe [user] message says \"Done. Both parts verified.\" with the manifest fix and clean run. This suggests the user did it, OR the user is reporting. Given the assistant \"cannot run pytest,\" it seems the user has shell access and can run things. Actually, the workspace is at `/home/iconbaypark2900/openworker-tasks/1472646f-019` which is writable, but agpack itself at `/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack` is read-only.\n\nHmm, but the user said the manifest was fixed \u2014 but agpack is read-only. So either the user has write access via some other means, or the \"read-only\" is from the assistant''s tool perspective. Let me not overstate. The key point: the assistant''s tools couldn''t write to agpack (read-only shell for the assistant), but the user made the change and ran pytest.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"[assistant] The user ''Done. Both parts verified.''\" \u2014 the manifest fix and the clean run were reported by the user. So the user did these actions (or had them done).\n\nLet me also note: the assistant could only read agpack, not execute commands there. The grep \"path escapes the workspace\" errors confirm the assistant''s grep/list tools are confined to the openworker-tasks workspace.\n\n**Files/artifacts:**\n\n1. `/home/iconbaypark2900/dataScience/agpack/pyproject.toml` \u2014 production manifest. Changed to