hown in the second tool result still shows ansi.js import. This is contradictory.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I''ll note in the summary that run.ts was being refactored to inline ANSI codes and drop the ansi.js shim dependency, and that there may be a leftover ansi.js file to reconcile. Actually, since I need to be concrete and not create confusion, I''ll state: \"run.ts refactored to inline ANSI escape codes; the ansi.js shim (created earlier) is being dropped as unused \u2014 verify no other file imports it.\"\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The safest is to note run.ts currently imports from ''./ansi.js'' (CONFORMANCE_OK, CONFORMANCE_WARN, reset), and the assistant intended to inline those codes and drop ansi.js. There''s a potential dangling ansi.js file to reconcile.\n\nLet me focus on what''s clearly established and load-bearing.\n\nNow, the key thing: The last action was writing validators.ts to fix contract-shape and settlement validators. So current work is finishing validators.ts.\n\nLet me note the terminal classes issue:\n- `FailureClass = ''blocked'' | ''payment-required'' | ''token-rejected''`\n- `TerminalKind` \u2014 the contract-shape validator gates on `fulfilled` because denied outcomes lack content/license/settlement.\n\nLet me think about the four terminal kinds. TerminalKind is likely ''fulfilled'' | ''denied'' | ''blocked'' | ... Let me check. From broker.ts, `TerminalKind` type. The dashboard/ledger renders terminal classes. The 4 terminal classes the broker reports \u2014 these are probably: fulfilled, denied, blocked, payment-required... Actually the validators mention \"4 terminal classes.\"\n\nFrom types.ts FailureClass: ''blocked'' | ''payment-required'' | ''token-rejected''. That''s 3. But the settlement validator checks \"4 terminal classes.\" Hmm. TerminalKind might be different. Let me note the settlement validator checks spend line sums across 4 terminal classes. I''ll just describe it.\n\nLet me not over-specify TerminalKind since I don''t have it precisely. I''ll say \"the four terminal classes (TerminalKind)\" and gate contract-shape/settlement checks on outcome === ''fulfilled''.\n\nNow let me also capture the CI gate details from the prompt summary at the top:\n- CI order, three gates that are green (typecheck, test, and one more?), packages/conformance/ is the sole outstanding failure, root conformance script points at nonexistent src/cli.ts.\n\nWait, actually the root conformance script points at a nonexistent src/cli.ts \u2014 but the assistant DID create src/cli.ts. So that gap is being closed. Let me note: the root package.json had a conformance script pointing at a nonexistent path; the assistant created packages/conformance/src/cli.ts to satisfy it.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. The top-level user message is actually the summary text (the big block). That''s the \"user\" message \u2014 it''s the task description essentially. So the primary request is embedded in that.\n\nLet me now write the summary.\n\nFor \"All user messages\" \u2014 there was effectively one user turn with that big block of text. I''ll list it.\n\nLet me structure everything.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the files structure. The requested structure per the prompt: package.json, registry, validators, run()/report, CLI, a PASS fixture, and a deliberately failing fixture. Plus index.\n\nSo still needed:\n- src/types.ts \u2713 (with replacements to add linkHref, settlementRef)\n- src/validators.ts \u2713 being finalized\n- src/registry.ts \u2713\n- src/run.ts \u2713 (report function)\n- src/cli.ts \u2713\n- src/index.ts \u2713\n- package.json \u2713\n- tsconfig.json \u2713\n- Still MISSING: fixtures (PASS and FAIL), and the ansi.js reconciliation.\n\nWait, the prompt says \"a PASS fixture, and a deliberately failing fixture\" \u2014 these are not yet created. Let me confirm: the todos were:\n1. Study the broker''s public contract surface (done)\n2. Create packages/conformance package.json, tsconfig.json, types, registry, validators, run, cli, index (in progress)\n3. Author PASS and FAIL fixtures derived from the real c... (pending)\n\nSo fixtures are pending.\n\nAlso the verification sequence (from the summary block): 38/38 still pass, PASS fixture exits 0, bad fixture exits 1 (proving the gate is real), typecheck stays green. But 38/38 refers to the existing test suite \u2014 the conformance package is new so it doesn''t add to that necessarily, or the verification is about running the gate.\n\nAlso: \"Output format \u2014 ready-to-paste blocks marked NEW vs EDIT\" \u2014 this is an output constraint for how to present the deliverable. So the final deliverable should be presented as paste-ready blocks marked NEW vs EDIT, grouped under packages/conformance/. But given this is a build task where files are actually written to the workspace, the \"output format\" might be for the summary/report to the user. I''ll note this constraint.\n\nWait \u2014 actually, looking at the actual work done, the assistant was writing files to the workspace (write_file tool). So the deliverable is real", "working_state": "## Working state (extracted mechanically from tool records)\nFiles written/edited (most recent first):\n- packages/conformance/src/validators.ts\n- packages/conformance/src/index.ts\n- packages/conformance/src/cli.ts\n- packages/conformance/src/run.ts\n- packages/conformance/src/registry.ts\n- packages/conformance/src/types.ts\n- packages/conformance/tsconfig.json\n- packages/conformance/package.json\nRecent shell commands:\n- cat /home/iconbaypark2900/openworker-tasks/6968e75b-227/metered-web-broker-conformance-prompt.md  [ok]\n- grep -l ''\"typecheck\"'' packages/*/package.json 2>/dev/null; echo ''--- scripts ---''; grep -h ''\"typecheck\"\\|\"test\"\\|\"conformance\"\\|\"demo\"'' packages/*/package.json   [ok]\n- npm run typecheck 2>&1 | tail -n 5; echo ''=== TYPECHECK EXIT:'' $?; npm test 2>&1 | tail -n 8; echo ''=== TEST EXIT:'' $?  [ok]\nTools used in the summarized span: g