_trust_audit.py (unknown_kind). And it''s about to make delegation test changes.\n\nBut wait \u2014 I need to double check: did the assistant actually apply the delegation.py scope fix? Let me look at the tool calls again. \n\nThe assistant said \"Let me apply the production changes first (audit, then delegation).\" then applied audit.py changes. Then ran audit. Then fixed test_trust_audit.py. Then updated todos. Then read delegation tests. Then grepped. Then read delegation test bodies. Then grep namespace constants.\n\nI do NOT see a replace_in_file on delegation.py in the visible tool calls. So the delegation.py scope fix has NOT been applied yet. The assistant only applied audit.py changes and test_trust_audit.py changes so far.\n\nHold on. Let me re-read more carefully. The diagnosis said \"Let me apply the production changes first (audit, then delegation).\" But the actual tool calls only show audit.py changes. So either:\n(a) The delegation.py scope fix is still pending, OR\n(b) It was applied but not shown.\n\nGiven the tool call sequence I can see, only audit.py test_trust_audit.py were modified. The delegation scope production fix appears pending. But actually, since the assistant said it would apply production changes \"first (audit, then delegation)\" and then got caught up in the audit fix (found unknown_kind was a test issue), then moved to reading delegation tests... \n\nActually, looking at the very end, the assistant is STILL reading delegation test files and confirming namespace constants. So it''s in the middle of the delegation diagnosis/fix phase. The delegation scope production fix and delegation test fixes have NOT been applied.\n\nHmm, but actually I realize I should reconsider whether the scope step-1 removal is really needed. Let me think about the actual failing tests.\n\nThe 5 failing delegation tests were:\n1. test_scope_closed_rejects_unrecognised\n2. test_chain_line_single_hop_root_is_root\n3. test_chain_line_tail_must_be_sub\n4. test_